Spinosaurus is probably the most  enigmatic dinosaur out there for decades  paleontologists have disagreed on how  Spinosaurus and Jim takus a huge  Cretaceous predator might have lived and  hunted now a new skeleton discovered in  Morocco has revealed an unexpected  surprise in the tail of this enormous  hunter we're digging up bones you can  imagine I've never seen anything like  this before the new bones suggest that  Spinosaurus swam through the water  propelled by a powerful tail a lifestyle  apparently unique among dinosaurs we can  finally flesh out this bizarre  mysterious dinosaur and it turns out it  was even stranger than we expected  so what you see here is the Sahara one  of the driest places on earth but a  hundred million years ago this was a  huge river system Nyssa Ibrahim wanted  to be a paleontologist since he was five  so a trip to the Sahara to uncover a new  Spinosaurus skeleton was a dream come  true  especially given the scarcity of  Spinosaurus bones we're finding bone  after bone after bone and I couldn't  believe it it was like being a  five-year-old all over again  the only other Spinosaurus skeleton that  had ever been found was destroyed in  World War two and so we had this  tantalizing clues we knew the  Spinosaurus looked really strange it had  a giant sail on its back and narrow jaws  like a crocodile and we knew it was very  big probably even bigger than t-rex but  there was about it we really didn't know  anything about the lifestyle of  Spinosaurus and it's detailed Anatomy a  particular mystery was the tale of a  Spinosaurus a part of his body only  known from a few fragmentary bones  previous reconstructions showed a long  thin tail similar to other theropod  dinosaurs but when nazar and his team  started excavating the tail of the new  specimen they knew they'd found  something special so we're excavating  caudal vertebrae the tail vertebrae and  there's a spine at the top writes the  neural spine we still haven't reached  the end no it's not that's really weird  this is a tail bone at the end of the  tail why on earth does it have such a  long spine yeah 52 centimeters almost 53  actually no other dinosaur has tail  bones like this and that's when we  realized that the tail was basically  like a giant fin Nizar suspected that  this fin would have made Spinosaurus an  adept swimmer  to test the idea he sent the  reconstructed shape to colleagues at  Harvard University where they crafted  plastic tails representing Spinosaurus  to other theropod dinosaurs a crocodile  underneath  they then measured the thrust and  efficiency of each tail shape as it  moved in the water the spinosaurus tail  really outperforms the other dinosaur  tails and it is comparable in many ways  with the tails of crocodiles and newts  so what the experiments showed was that  this was a clear advantage for an animal  pursuing prey in the water spinosaurus  aquatic lifestyle has been the subject  of decades of debate while there were  several other large aquatic reptiles in  this period  none were dinosaurs for a very very long  time  paleontologists assume the one thing  dinosaurs never did was invade the  watery world aquatic ecosystems there  were already several pieces of evidence  pointing towards Spinosaurus being  adapted for water crocodile like teeth  for grasping fish dense bones for  buoyancy control and short hind legs  unsuited to running still researchers  disagreed on just how reliant  Spinosaurus was on aquatic prey and  whether it swam or just waded in the  shallows Nyssa believes this new find  could settle the debate the big thing we  were missing was a propulsive structure  because you can't really be an aquatic  predator unless you have some way to  catch prey in the water and move through  the water and so that's what we now  found we now know that this dinosaur had  a thin like tail that propelled it  through this river system so this was a  River monster it was a dinosaur that did  things that no other dinosaur did  paleontologists knew that dinosaurs went  on to conquer the skies but this is the  strongest evidence yet that at least one  of them also conquered the waters  it could be that the spinosaurus is  unique or it could show just how much  there is left to learn about the age of  dinosaurs  [Music]
 

